---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import TectonicsSim from '../../simulators/tectonics/TectonicsSim.astro';
import { tectonicsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/tectonics';

const toc = [
  { id: 'plates', label: 'Earth’s moving plates' },
  { id: 'drive', label: 'What moves the plates' },
  { id: 'divergent', label: 'Divergent boundaries' },
  { id: 'convergent', label: 'Convergent boundaries' },
  { id: 'transform', label: 'Transform boundaries' },
  { id: 'evidence', label: 'The evidence' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Kious, W. J., & Tilling, R. I. (1996). This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics. U.S. Geological Survey.', url: 'https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/dynamic.html' },
  { text: 'Wells, D. L., & Coppersmith, K. J. (1994). New empirical relationships among magnitude, rupture length, rupture width, rupture area, and surface displacement. Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 84(4), 974–1002.' },
  { text: 'Parsons, B., & Sclater, J. G. (1977). An analysis of the variation of ocean floor bathymetry and heat flow with age. Journal of Geophysical Research, 82(5), 803–827.' },
  { text: 'Vine, F. J., & Matthews, D. H. (1963). Magnetic anomalies over oceanic ridges. Nature, 199, 947–949.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="earth-science"
  topic="plate-tectonics"
  description="Interactive plate tectonics simulator: watch sea-floor spreading with magnetic stripes, subduction zones and volcanoes, colliding continents building mountains, and earthquakes on a transform fault."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={tectonicsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      The ground beneath your feet is moving, about as fast as your fingernails grow. Earth’s outer shell is broken into
      giant plates that pull apart, crash together and grind past one another, building mountains, opening oceans and
      causing earthquakes and volcanoes. Choose a boundary and fast-forward millions of years.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <TectonicsSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>plate boundary</strong>, then watch it change. In the cross-sections, one second is one million years.</li>
      <li><strong>Pulling apart:</strong> see new sea floor form at the ridge, with magnetic stripes recording reversals of Earth’s field.</li>
      <li><strong>Ocean meets continent:</strong> watch the ocean plate sink, earthquakes along the slab and a volcanic mountain range grow.</li>
      <li><strong>Continents collide:</strong> watch the crust fold and rise into mountains with a deep root.</li>
      <li><strong>Sliding past:</strong> a map view (one second is 20 years). Watch the roads bend as strain builds, then snap in an earthquake.</li>
      <li>Change the <strong>plate speed</strong> and compare how quickly features form.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="plates">Earth’s moving plates</h2>
  <p>
    Earth’s rigid outer layer, the <strong>lithosphere</strong> (the crust plus the top of the mantle), is broken into
    about 15 major <strong>tectonic plates</strong> and many smaller ones. They float on the
    <strong>asthenosphere</strong>, a hot, slowly flowing layer of the mantle, and move a few centimetres a year. There are
    two kinds of crust: thin, dense <strong>oceanic crust</strong> made of basalt, and thick, lighter
    <strong>continental crust</strong> rich in granite. Most earthquakes and volcanoes happen where plates meet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="drive">What moves the plates</h2>
  <p>
    Heat from Earth’s interior keeps the mantle churning in slow <strong>convection currents</strong>: hot rock rises, cools
    and sinks again over millions of years. The plates are also pulled along by their own cold, heavy edges sinking at
    subduction zones (<strong>slab pull</strong>) and pushed away from high mid-ocean ridges (<strong>ridge push</strong>).
    Slab pull is thought to be the largest force.
  </p>

  <h2 id="divergent">Divergent boundaries</h2>
  <p>
    Where plates move apart, hot mantle rock rises, partly melts and erupts to form new crust. Under the oceans this
    builds <strong>mid-ocean ridges</strong>, a 65,000 km mountain chain winding around the globe. As the new sea floor
    moves away from the ridge it cools, becomes denser and sinks deeper, so the ocean is shallow at the ridge and deep far
    from it. On land, divergence creates <strong>rift valleys</strong> such as the East African Rift, which may one day
    become a new ocean.
  </p>

  <h2 id="convergent">Convergent boundaries</h2>
  <p>
    Where plates collide, what happens depends on the crust involved. When ocean meets continent, the denser ocean plate is
    forced down in <strong>subduction</strong>, forming a deep <strong>trench</strong>. Water released from the sinking
    slab lowers the melting point of the mantle above, creating magma that feeds explosive volcanoes such as those of the
    Andes. Ocean–ocean subduction builds volcanic island arcs such as Japan. When two continents meet, neither sinks:
    the crust thickens into great <strong>fold mountains</strong> such as the Himalayas and the Alps.
  </p>

  <h2 id="transform">Transform boundaries</h2>
  <p>
    Where plates slide past each other sideways, crust is neither made nor destroyed. Friction locks the fault, so the
    rocks on either side bend as the plates keep moving. When the stored strain overcomes friction, the fault slips
    suddenly in an <strong>earthquake</strong> and the rocks snap back: this is the <strong>elastic rebound theory</strong>,
    developed after the 1906 San Francisco earthquake. The San Andreas Fault moves about 3–5 cm a year on average, but in
    sudden jumps of several metres.
  </p>

  <h2 id="evidence">The evidence</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fitting coastlines and fossils:</strong> Alfred Wegener noticed in 1912 that South America and Africa fit together and share fossils such as <em>Mesosaurus</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Magnetic stripes:</strong> symmetric bands of normal and reversed magnetism on either side of ridges (Vine and Matthews, 1963).</li>
    <li><strong>Sea-floor age:</strong> the youngest rock is at the ridges; no ocean floor is older than about 200 million years.</li>
    <li><strong>GPS:</strong> today satellites measure plate motions directly, to the millimetre.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Lithosphere / asthenosphere</dt>
    <dd>The rigid outer shell broken into plates / the soft, slowly flowing mantle layer beneath it.</dd>
    <dt>Subduction</dt>
    <dd>One plate sinking beneath another into the mantle.</dd>
    <dt>Fault</dt>
    <dd>A fracture where rocks on either side have moved.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Plates float on an ocean of liquid magma.”</strong> The mantle is almost entirely solid rock that flows extremely slowly.</li>
    <li><strong>“Continents move but the ocean floor doesn’t.”</strong> Plates carry both; ocean floor is constantly made and destroyed.</li>
    <li><strong>“Earthquakes only happen at plate boundaries.”</strong> Most do, but some occur inside plates on old faults.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Earth’s lithosphere is divided into moving plates driven by mantle convection and slab pull.</li>
    <li>Divergent boundaries make new crust; convergent boundaries destroy or crumple it; transform boundaries slide past.</li>
    <li>Plate boundaries are where most earthquakes, volcanoes and mountain ranges form.</li>
    <li>Magnetic stripes, matching fossils and GPS measurements all support plate tectonics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: cross-sections are schematic with exaggerated vertical scale. Sea-floor depth uses the age–depth relation d ≈ 2.5 km + 0.35 km × √age. Earthquake magnitudes on the transform fault are estimated from the slip using the Wells and Coppersmith relationship.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
